      <h2>Setup &amp; Assembly</h2>

      <div class="warning">
        Disconnect the lathe from power during assembly. The lathe weighs 187 lbs; use an assistant or a hoist to lift.
      </div>

      <ol>
        <li>Remove any screws or straps holding the lathe to the pallet and remove protective wrapping. Set accessory boxes aside.</li>
        <li>Place the lathe in a dry, well-lit area on a sturdy bench or the Powermatic stand, with room around it for operation and maintenance.</li>
        <li>Exposed metal (bedways, spindles) is factory-coated with a protective film. Remove it with a soft cloth and cleaner/degreaser, including the bed areas under the headstock, tailstock and tool rest base. Do not use abrasive pads; keep solvents off painted and plastic surfaces.</li>
        <li>Apply a light coat of SAE 10 oil to exposed metal to prevent rust.</li>
        <li><strong>Tool caddy:</strong> mount it to the lathe bed with the provided screws and washers. It can also be mounted to the 13&quot; bed extension.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>13&quot; Bed Extension (PM1-485, sold separately)</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Slide the tailstock away from the end of the bed and unscrew the stud (A).</li>
        <li>With an assistant holding the extension flush to the end of the bed, insert the four 3/8 x 1-3/4 socket head cap screws with lock and flat washers (supplied with the extension) through the extension into the threaded holes in the lathe bed. Snug but do not fully tighten.</li>
        <li>Align the top surface and inside ways of the extension with the lathe bed as closely as possible.</li>
        <li>Slide the tailstock over the joint so its clamping nut is centered on the joint, then firmly tighten the tailstock clamping handle. This aligns the two beds.</li>
        <li>Tighten the four screws securely.</li>
        <li>Unlock the tailstock and slide it back and forth across the joint to test smoothness; readjust if needed.</li>
        <li>Install the stud at the end of the extension.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p11_00.jpg" alt="Bed extension bolted to the end of the lathe bed with the tailstock straddling the joint" />
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p11_01.jpg" alt="Bed extension mounted in the lower position for outboard bowl turning, with tool rest post extension" />
      </div>
      <p>If the lathe is on the Powermatic stand, the extension may instead be mounted in the <strong>lower position</strong> for bowl turning with the headstock at the outboard end of the bed. A tool rest post extension (B) is included with the extension to raise the tool rest when used in the lower position.</p>

      <h3>Stand Installation</h3>
      <p>The stand is included with PM1-482. For PM1-483 (lathe only), the PM1-484 stand can be added.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Screw the levelers into the threaded holes on the bottom of each leg and tighten the hex nut against the leg to lock each leveler.
          <div class="figure">
            <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p11_02.jpg" alt="Leveler foot threaded into the bottom of a stand leg with its jam nut" />
            <figcaption>Figure 6-4: Levelers</figcaption>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>Lay the two stand base sections flat, inside facing up. Each side has two threaded mounting holes (A) for a leg.</li>
        <li>Attach each leg to the stand base using <strong>both</strong> mounting holes and two bolts, each with a washer and lock washer. Use the same pair of holes on every leg so the lathe sits level. Tighten firmly.
          <div class="figure">
            <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p12_01.jpg" alt="Stand leg bolted to the base casting through two mounting holes; legs are marked L and R" />
            <figcaption>Figure 6-5: Leg to base mounting</figcaption>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>The top of each base section has four mounting holes (B). Align them with the holes in the lathe bed. Load each 5/16&quot; bolt with a lock washer and flat washer, insert through the base into the threaded bed holes, and tighten firmly.
          <div class="figure">
            <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p12_02.jpg" alt="Stand base top showing four bolt holes aligned with the lathe bed mounting holes" />
            <figcaption>Figure 6-6: Lathe to stand mounting</figcaption>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="danger">
        <strong>Each leg must be attached with two bolts through two base holes.</strong> Never use a single hole. The legs are side-specific, marked &ldquo;L&rdquo; and &ldquo;R&rdquo;, and must be oriented with the levelers flat on the floor. An incorrectly assembled stand is unstable and could collapse.
      </div>

      <h3>Headstock Utility Holes</h3>
      <p>Two M12-1.5 threaded holes atop the headstock, covered by rubber bungs, accept accessories such as lighting, guarding, or dust collection. Remove a bung with a flat-blade screwdriver. Keep the holes covered when not in use so debris does not enter the headstock.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p12_03.jpg" alt="Top of the headstock showing the two rubber-bunged utility holes" />
        <figcaption>Figure 6-7: Utility holes</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h2>Electrical Connections</h2>
      <div class="danger">
        Electrical connections must be made by a qualified electrician in compliance with all relevant codes. The machine must be properly grounded to prevent electrical shock and possible fatal injury.
      </div>
      <p>The lathe runs on single-phase 120V. The variable frequency drive converts incoming power to drive the 3-phase motor. Connect to a 15 A circuit with a circuit breaker or time-delay fuse; local codes take precedence.</p>

      <h3>Grounding</h3>
      <p>The cord has an equipment-grounding conductor (green, with or without yellow stripes) and a grounding plug that must go into a matching, properly grounded outlet. Do not modify the plug; if it will not fit, have the proper outlet installed. If repairing the cord or plug, never connect the grounding conductor to a live terminal. Use only 3-wire extension cords with 3-prong plugs and 3-pole receptacles.</p>
      <p>The plug resembles sketch A below. A temporary adapter (sketches B and C) may be used with a 2-pole receptacle only until a grounded outlet can be installed; its green ear or lug must be connected to a permanent ground and the adapter held in place by a metal screw. Temporary adapters are not permitted in Canada.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p13_01.jpg" alt="Three sketches: grounded 3-prong plug and outlet, and a grounding adapter on a 2-pole receptacle" />
        <figcaption>Figure 7-1: Plug configuration</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Extension Cords</h3>
      <p>Extension cords are discouraged. If one is unavoidable, it must be in good condition and heavy enough for the current drawn; an undersized cord drops voltage, loses power and overheats. When in doubt use the next heavier gauge.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Amp Rating</th><th>25 ft</th><th>50 ft</th><th>100 ft</th><th>150 ft</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>0&ndash;6 A</td><td>18 AWG</td><td>16 AWG</td><td>16 AWG</td><td>14 AWG</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6&ndash;10 A</td><td>18 AWG</td><td>16 AWG</td><td>14 AWG</td><td>12 AWG</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10&ndash;12 A</td><td>16 AWG</td><td>16 AWG</td><td>14 AWG</td><td>12 AWG</td></tr>
          <tr><td>12&ndash;16 A</td><td>14 AWG</td><td>12 AWG</td><td colspan="2">Not recommended</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Adjustments</h2>

      <h3>Headstock, Tailstock &amp; Tool Rest</h3>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p14_00.jpg" alt="Headstock, tailstock and tool rest base with lettered callouts A through F for the locking handles, stud, quill handle and handwheel" />
        <figcaption>Figure 8-1: Headstock, tailstock, tool support</figcaption>
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Sliding:</strong> loosen the locking handle (A) until the headstock or tailstock slides freely; swing the handle tight once positioned.</li>
        <li><strong>Removing:</strong> unscrew the stud (B) at either end of the bed. Always reinstall the stud after remounting.</li>
        <li><strong>Inboard turning:</strong> headstock stays at the left end; move only the tailstock. Loosen handle (C) to advance or retract the quill with the handwheel (D); retighten (C) and lock the tailstock before operating.</li>
        <li><strong>Outboard turning:</strong> remove the tailstock and tool rest assembly and slide the headstock to the opposite end of the bed. Lock it before operating.</li>
        <li><strong>Tool rest:</strong> loosen handle (E) to slide and angle the base; loosen the small handle (F) to set tool rest height and angle, making sure the clamp bushings are spread enough to accept the post. Tighten both before operating. Handle (F) and the bushings can be swapped to the opposite side of the base.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p14_01.jpg" alt="Headstock moved to the right end of the bed for outboard turning" />
        <figcaption>Figure 8-2: Outboard turning position</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Cam Tightness</h3>
      <p>If the headstock, tailstock or tool rest base will not clamp down firmly when locked, remove the stud, slide the assembly off the bed, turn it on its side and tighten the cam lock nut with a wrench. Remount, test, and reinsert the stud.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p14_02.jpg" alt="Tailstock turned on its side showing the cam lock nut being tightened" />
        <figcaption>Figure 8-3: Cam adjustment</figcaption>
      </div>
      <p><strong>Locking handles</strong> such as (C) and (F) can be repositioned: lift the handle, rotate it on its pin, and release so it reseats.</p>

      <h3>Indexer</h3>
      <p>The indexer has 24 holes (15&deg; apart) around the spindle for cutting evenly spaced features such as flutes while the spindle is locked. Numbers 1&ndash;24 are inscribed on the top sheave (K). With power on, the LED readout can locate positions quickly:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Connect the lathe to power.</li>
        <li>Press the index button (G); the readout switches from RPM to index position.</li>
        <li>Rotate the workpiece to the desired position with the handwheel (H).</li>
        <li>Push in the index pin (J) and rock the workpiece slightly until the pin engages, then screw the pin further in to lock the spindle.</li>
        <li>Cut the first flute.</li>
        <li>Press the index button (G) to zero the current position.</li>
        <li>Unscrew the pin, rotate to the next desired position on the readout, re-engage the pin, and cut. Repeat.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Example: for 6 evenly spaced flutes, use readout positions 0, 8, 16, 24, 32 and 40.</p>
      <div class="warning">
        Unscrew and release the index pin before turning the spindle on. The readout reverts to RPM once the spindle starts.
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p15_00.jpg" alt="Headstock with the index pin, handwheel, index button and numbered top sheave labeled" />
        <figcaption>Figure 8-4: Indexer</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Centers</h3>
      <p><strong>Headstock spur center:</strong> drive the spur center into the workpiece first (see Spindle Turning), clean its taper and the inside of the spindle, and push it in. To remove, take the workpiece off the lathe, pass the knockout rod through the handwheel and tap the center out. Catch it with thumb and forefinger or a rag so it does not fall and get damaged.</p>
      <p><strong>Tailstock live center:</strong> clean the taper and quill bore and push the center in; it should resist being pulled out by hand. To remove, loosen the quill lock (C) and retract the quill with the handwheel until the center self-ejects, holding it so it does not drop.</p>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p15_01.jpg" alt="Knockout rod inserted through the headstock handwheel to eject the spur center" />
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p15_02.jpg" alt="Live center being pushed into the tailstock quill" />
      </div>
      <p>A #2 Morse taper drill chuck (not provided) can be mounted in the tailstock for end drilling. The tailstock through-hole allows deep-hole drilling with a hollow center and auger (not provided), as for a lamp spindle.</p>

      <h3>Faceplate</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Disconnect the lathe from power and mount the faceplate to the bowl blank.</li>
        <li>Pull up the spindle lock tab and rotate the handwheel until the lock engages. Hold the tab up.</li>
        <li>Thread the faceplate onto the spindle clockwise, hand tight. Forward rotation will snug it further.</li>
        <li>Release the tab and rotate the spindle slightly to confirm the lock has disengaged.</li>
        <li>To remove, back off the two set screws with a 3mm hex key, engage the spindle lock, and turn the faceplate counterclockwise with the faceplate wrench.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="warning">
        If you will run the spindle in reverse, tighten both faceplate set screws first or the faceplate can unscrew from the spindle. Always back the set screws off before removing the faceplate or the spindle threads can be damaged.
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p16_02.jpg" alt="Spindle lock tab raised and faceplate wrench on the faceplate; one set screw indicated" />
        <figcaption>Figure 8-7: Installing/removing faceplate</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Speed Change (Belt Position)</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Disconnect from power and open the headstock access door.</li>
        <li>Loosen the pivot lock handle (L), lift the tension handle (M) to raise the motor, and retighten (L) to hold it up.</li>
        <li>Move the belt to the step shown on the door label for the speed range you want.</li>
        <li>Loosen (L) and lower the motor to tension the belt. Seat the V-grooves properly. Do not over-tension; very light pressure on (M) is enough to prevent slipping.</li>
        <li>Tighten (L).</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p16_03.png" alt="Belt position graphic showing the three sheave steps and their speed ranges" />
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p16_04.jpg" alt="Inside the headstock: spindle sheave set screw N, pivot lock handle L, tension handle M, handwheel set screw P" />
      </div>

      <h3>Sheave &amp; Belt Alignment</h3>
      <p>If any service disturbs the alignment of the motor and spindle sheaves, loosen the two set screws on the spindle sheave (N), nudge the sheave into line, and retighten. Properly aligned sheaves run without pulsing sounds or belt noise.</p>

      <h3>Sheave / Drive Belt Replacement</h3>
      <div class="note">
        This can be a challenging procedure. If you are not confident, take the headstock to an authorized Powermatic service center.
      </div>
      <ol>
        <li>Disconnect from power. Loosen (L), lift (M) to raise the motor, tighten (L). Slip the belt off the lower pulley.</li>
        <li>Loosen the two handwheel set screws (P) with a 3mm hex key and unscrew the handwheel. <strong>Left-hand thread: rotate clockwise to loosen.</strong></li>
        <li>Tap the left end of the spindle with a rubber mallet, or a steel hammer against a wood block, to shift it right just enough to free the sheave or belt. Never strike the spindle directly with steel. Do not let the top sheave be forced into the sensor/encoder assembly; loosen its set screws and slide it left if needed.</li>
        <li>If replacing the sheave, loosen set screws (N), slide it off, and install the new one loosely in the correct orientation.</li>
        <li>Push the spindle back into place, making sure the sensor collar sits over the sensor so the digital readout works.</li>
        <li>Check for spindle play or runout; see Troubleshooting if present.</li>
        <li>Reinstall the handwheel and tighten (P). Align the sheave, then tighten (N) securely.</li>
        <li>Loosen (L), lower the motor onto the belt with light tension, and tighten (L).</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Leg Height Adjustment</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Disconnect from power.</li>
        <li>Support the lathe bed with chocks, jacks or another support able to bear its full weight. Make sure it is balanced and cannot tip.</li>
        <li>Remove the two bolts, washers and lock washers from each leg.</li>
        <li>Set the new height and reattach each leg with both bolts, washers and lock washers. Tighten firmly.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p17_01.png" alt="Lathe on stand with legs at their lowest setting" />
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p17_02.png" alt="Lathe on stand with legs at their highest setting" />
      </div>

      <h2>Operating Controls</h2>

      <h3>Main Power Switch</h3>
      <p>On the side of the inverter cover. Turning it ON powers the remote switch box; it does not start the motor. The flange holes (A) accept a padlock.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p18_00.jpg" alt="Main ON/OFF rocker switch on the inverter cover with padlock holes indicated" />
        <figcaption>Figure 9-1: ON/OFF switch</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Magnetic Remote Switch Box</h3>
      <div class="warning">
        Turn the main power switch OFF before repositioning the remote box. Moving it with power on can cause an unintended start.
      </div>
      <p>The box sticks magnetically anywhere on the lathe body.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>(A) Start/Stop:</strong> green starts the spindle, red stops it.</li>
        <li><strong>(B) Direction:</strong> can be flipped while running; the spindle slows to a stop before reversing. Confirm both faceplate set screws are tight before reversing.</li>
        <li><strong>(C) Speed dial:</strong> always start with the dial fully counterclockwise (lowest speed).</li>
        <li><strong>(D) Index button:</strong> zeroes the index readout (see Indexer).</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p18_02.jpg" alt="Remote control box with digital readout, start/stop buttons, forward/reverse switch, speed dial and index button labeled A through D" />
        <figcaption>Figure 9-2: Operating controls</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Variable Frequency Drive</h3>
      <p>The inverter provides infinitely variable speed within each belt range, a soft-start acceleration ramp, and braking to shorten coast-down. It is factory programmed and needs no setup; use only the switch box controls. If an inverter error occurs, contact Powermatic Technical Service at 800-274-6846.</p>
      <div class="note">
        A lightning strike or power surge can destroy the inverter. Unplug the lathe when not in use, or have a 3-pole disconnect installed on the supply side.
      </div>

      <h2>Operation</h2>

      <h3>Pre-Operation Inspection</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Level</strong> the machine with the stand&rsquo;s leveling feet.</li>
        <li><strong>Bearings:</strong> no end play or spindle runout.</li>
        <li><strong>Belt:</strong> snug, not overly tight.</li>
        <li><strong>Bedways:</strong> keep clean; remove wet shavings immediately, especially after turning green bowl blanks. Remove rust spots with steel wool and apply paste wax.</li>
        <li><strong>Tool rest:</strong> file off nicks and dings.</li>
        <li><strong>Spindle tapers:</strong> clean and free of dust and chips.</li>
        <li><strong>Tailstock:</strong> clean and lubricate the quill and lock.</li>
        <li><strong>Lighting:</strong> adequate to eliminate shadows.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Turning Tools</h3>
      <p>Choose quality high-speed steel tools; they hold an edge far longer than carbon steel. A basic kit:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Skews</strong> 1-1/2&quot; and 1&quot; or 1-1/4&quot;: finishing cuts and details</li>
        <li><strong>Large roughing gouge</strong> 1&quot;&ndash;1-1/4&quot;: removing waste</li>
        <li><strong>Spindle gouges</strong> 1/4&quot;, 3/8&quot;, 1/2&quot;: beads, coves, details</li>
        <li><strong>Deep-fluted bowl gouges</strong> 1/4&quot;, 3/8&quot;, 1/2&quot;: bowls and plates</li>
        <li><strong>Square scraper (bedan)</strong> 3/8&quot; or 1/2&quot;: square shoulders</li>
        <li><strong>Large round-nose scraper</strong> 1-1/2&quot;: smoothing bowl interiors and edges</li>
        <li><strong>Parting tool</strong> 1/8&quot;: cut-offs and sizing cuts</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p19_00.jpg" alt="Line drawings of the basic turning tools: skews, roughing gouge, spindle gouges, bowl gouge, scrapers and parting tool" />
        <figcaption>Figure 10-1: Basic turning tools</figcaption>
      </div>
      <p><strong>Sharpening:</strong> a slow-speed (1800 RPM) grinder with 60-grit (shaping) and 100-grit (final edge) aluminum oxide wheels, kept true with a diamond dresser. Keep the tool moving with a light touch. Carbon steel that turns blue has lost its temper and must be ground past the blue. Do not quench HSS. Honing with a diamond lap or slipstone extends time between grindings.</p>

      <h3>Spindle Turning</h3>
      <p>Work is held between a spur or drive center in the headstock and a live center in the tailstock. A cup center in the tailstock reduces the risk of splitting compared with a cone center.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p20_00.jpg" alt="Basic spindle profile shapes: beads, coves, V-cuts and fillets" />
        <figcaption>Figure 10-2: Basic spindle profiles</figcaption>
      </div>
      <p><strong>Stock:</strong> straight-grained, free of checks, cracks and knots, cut 1/8&quot;&ndash;1/4&quot; oversize in diameter with extra length for the ends. Knock the corners off large stock to make an octagon.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Find and mark center on both ends with a combination square or center finder. Dimple it with an awl or center punch. Accuracy matters most where square sections will remain.</li>
        <li>Very hard woods may need saw kerfs cut across the ends so the spur center can bite.
          <div class="figure">
            <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p20_01.png" alt="Diagonal saw kerfs cut in the end of a spindle blank for the spur center" />
            <figcaption>Figure 10-3: Kerfs for hard stock</figcaption>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>Drive the spur center about 1/4&quot; into the blank with a wood mallet or dead-blow hammer. Never use a steel hammer, and never drive the blank onto a spur center that is mounted in the lathe.
          <div class="figure">
            <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p20_02.png" alt="Mallet driving the spur center into the end of a blank on the bench" />
            <figcaption>Figure 10-4: Seating the spur center</figcaption>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>Confirm the headstock is locked to the bed. Clean the spur center taper and spindle bore, and insert the center with the blank attached.</li>
        <li>Support the blank while bringing the tailstock up to about 1&quot; from its end; lock the tailstock.</li>
        <li>Advance the quill with the handwheel to seat the live center. Use enough pressure to hold the work securely, no more. Tighten the quill lock.</li>
        <li>Set the tool rest parallel to the work, just below center, 1/8&quot;&ndash;1/4&quot; from the corners. Lock the base.</li>
        <li>Rotate the blank by hand to check clearance.</li>
        <li>Start at the lowest speed and bring up to the appropriate RPM for the blank size, reading the digital readout.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="warning">
        Excessive tailstock pressure can overheat center bearings and damage the workpiece and the lathe. Apply only enough force to hold the work.
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p21_01.png" alt="Tool rest positioned just below center and close to the square corners of the blank" />
        <figcaption>Figure 10-5: Tool rest position</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h4>Roughing Out</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Rest the heel of a large roughing gouge on the tool rest and slowly raise the handle until the edge touches.</li>
        <li>Starting about 2&quot; from the tailstock end, roll the flute toward the direction of cut and make long sweeping passes to bring the blank to a cylinder.</li>
        <li>Keep as much of the bevel as possible rubbing to maintain control and avoid catches. Always cut downhill, large diameter to small, working toward the end of the piece and never starting at an end.</li>
        <li>Smooth the cylinder with a large skew held perpendicular to the spindle, using only the center third of its edge. Touching a point of the skew to the work will catch.</li>
        <li>Add details with skew, parting tool, scraper or spindle gouge.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p21_02.png" alt="Roughing gouge rolled into the cut with the bevel rubbing on a spinning blank" />
        <figcaption>Figure 10-6: Roughing out</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h4>Beads, Coves, V-Cuts &amp; Parting</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Beads:</strong> make a parting cut to depth on each side of the bead, then with a small skew or spindle gouge start at the center and cut down each side, rolling the tool in the direction of cut.</li>
        <li><strong>Coves:</strong> with a spindle gouge, flute at 90&deg; to the work, touch the point and roll into the bottom of the cove; stop at the bottom. Move over the cove width and repeat from the other side with the flute facing the opposite way. Going up the far side will catch.</li>
        <li><strong>V-cuts:</strong> use the long point of the skew. Lightly mark the center, then with the bevel parallel to the right side of the V, raise the handle and push to depth. Repeat from the left. Do not press the point straight in; it burnishes and tears fibers.</li>
        <li><strong>Parting off:</strong> lower the RPM, place the parting tool on the rest and raise the handle to cut toward center. Loosely cradle the piece in one hand as it separates.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p22_00.png" alt="Spindle gouge rolling into a cove from one side" />
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p22_01.png" alt="Skew long point making one side of a V-cut" />
      </div>

      <h4>Sanding &amp; Finishing Spindles</h4>
      <p>Clean cuts minimize sanding. Move the tool rest away, run at low speed, and start no coarser than 120 grit, progressing through every grit without skipping. Fold the paper into a pad; never wrap it around fingers or the work. Apply finish with the lathe stopped, wipe off excess, let dry, sand with 320 or 400, apply a second coat and buff.</p>

      <h3>Faceplate &amp; Bowl Turning</h3>
      <p>Faceplate work is normally done inboard over the bed. Larger pieces are turned outboard with the tailstock and tool rest base removed and the headstock at the far end of the bed.</p>

      <h4>Mounting Stock</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Choose stock at least 1/8&quot;&ndash;1/4&quot; larger in every dimension than the finished piece.</li>
        <li>Use the largest faceplate the piece allows.</li>
        <li>True one face for mounting against the faceplate.</li>
        <li>Mark and drill pilot holes using the faceplate as a template. Faceplates are drilled for #12 screws; Phillips or square-drive sheet metal screws hold better than slotted wood screws.</li>
        <li>If screws would intrude into the finished piece, glue a waste block the diameter of the faceplate to the blank. Both surfaces must be flat. Avoid paper joints; a catch with a bowl gouge can separate them. Dry stock bonds with white or yellow glue under clamps; green stock needs cyanoacrylate.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p23_00.png" alt="Faceplate screwed to a waste block that is glued to the bowl blank" />
        <figcaption>Figure 10-9: Faceplate mounting with a waste block</figcaption>
      </div>
      <p><strong>Faceplate or chuck?</strong> Faceplates are the simplest and most reliable holding method. A four-jaw scroll chuck with interchangeable jaws (most include a screw chuck) is convenient when working several pieces, since swapping work needs no screws. Consider the kinds of turning you plan to do before buying one.</p>
      <p><strong>Wood selection:</strong> firewood is cheap practice stock. Start with dry wood; move to green wood, which cuts easily, as skill grows. Green wood checks and cracks: leave logs long, trim 2&ndash;3&quot; off checked ends, and split along the pith since most checks radiate from it. Keep bowl walls a consistent thickness so the piece dries evenly. Distortion in green wood varies by species (fruitwoods move most), season of cutting, and storage.</p>
      <p><strong>Bowl tools:</strong> the deep-fluted bowl gouge is the essential tool. A 3/8&quot; or 1/2&quot; handles most bowls; 1/4&quot; suits small bowls and light finishing cuts; 3/4&quot; and 1&quot; are for very large work. A lightly tilted large domed scraper cleans up interior ridges.</p>

      <h4>Shaping the Outside</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>On irregular blanks (burls, crotches) remove bark from what will be the top center.</li>
        <li>Drive the spur center into the top with a mallet, mount it in the headstock, bring up the tailstock with a live center, lock the tailstock and seat the center. Tighten the quill lock.</li>
        <li>Turn by hand to check clearance. Start at lowest speed and bring it up slowly, watching for vibration. If the lathe shakes, slow down.</li>
        <li>Rough the outside with a 1/2&quot; bowl gouge, handle braced against your hip, moving the tool with your whole body.</li>
        <li>As the shape develops, work the bottom (tailstock end) to accept a faceplate. Turn a short tenon about 1/8&quot; long sized to the faceplate&rsquo;s center hole for self-centering (or a tenon sized to your chuck).
          <div class="figure">
            <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p24_00.png" alt="Bowl blank between centers with a short centering tenon turned on its base" />
            <figcaption>Figure 10-10: Centering tenon</figcaption>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>Stop the lathe, remove the blank and attach the faceplate or chuck. Faceplate and blank must mate flush.</li>
        <li>Finish the outside with a 1/2&quot; or 3/8&quot; gouge, leaving extra material at the base to support the piece while hollowing.</li>
      </ol>

      <h4>Shaping the Interior</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Stop the lathe and move the tailstock away (removing its center keeps it out of your elbow&rsquo;s way).</li>
        <li>Set the tool rest across the face just below center, at a right angle to the ways. Check clearance by hand.</li>
        <li>Face off the top with a light shearing cut from rim to center.</li>
        <li>Place a 1/2&quot; bowl gouge at center with the flute facing the top of the bowl, handle level and pointed toward four o&rsquo;clock. Control the edge with the left hand while the right swings the handle toward your body, rotating the flute upward as the cut deepens. Work progressively out toward the rim, turning the tool rest into the bowl as needed. Aim for one light continuous sweep from rim to bottom.
          <div class="figure">
            <img src="/manuals/powermatic-2014b/p24_02.png" alt="Bowl gouge entering the bowl interior at center with the handle toward four o&rsquo;clock" />
            <figcaption>Figure 10-11: Hollowing the interior</figcaption>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>Establish wall thickness at the rim and carry it down. Once the bottom is thin you cannot thin the rim.</li>
        <li>Move the rest outside to define the foot; a base about 1/3 the bowl diameter is a good rule. Work the area near the faceplate with a 1/4&quot; gouge.</li>
        <li>Begin the parting cut at the base but do not go all the way through yet.</li>
      </ol>

      <h4>Sanding &amp; Finishing Bowls</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Remove the tool rest and slow to about 500 RPM; high speed builds heat and can check some woods.</li>
        <li>Start at 120 grit with light pressure and step through each grit to 220. Power-sanding avoids concentric scratches. Keep rim and foot details crisp.</li>
        <li>Remove dust, apply the first coat of finish with the lathe off, wipe excess, let dry, sand with 320 or 400.</li>
        <li>Continue the parting cut to about 3&quot; remaining, then stop and finish the separation with a fine-tooth hand saw.</li>
        <li>Apply a second coat, dry, and buff.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Recommended Speeds</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Work Diameter</th><th>Balancing / Roughing</th><th>General Cutting</th><th>Finishing</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Up to 6&quot;</td><td>400&ndash;900 RPM</td><td>1500&ndash;3000 RPM</td><td>2000&ndash;3000 RPM</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6&quot;&ndash;10&quot;</td><td>250&ndash;400 RPM</td><td>600&ndash;900 RPM</td><td>800&ndash;1100 RPM</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10&quot;&ndash;14&quot;</td><td>200&ndash;300 RPM</td><td>400&ndash;700 RPM</td><td>500&ndash;900 RPM</td></tr>
          <tr><td>14&quot;+</td><td>150&ndash;250 RPM</td><td>300&ndash;500 RPM</td><td>500&ndash;700 RPM</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>A general guideline only. If the lathe starts to shake or walk, reduce RPM immediately. For 14&quot;+ or unbalanced stock, adding weight to the lathe to damp vibration is recommended.</p>

      <h2>Maintenance</h2>
      <div class="warning">
        Unplug the lathe or switch off the supply before any maintenance.
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li>Clean the bed, faceplate and spindle nose with a cleaner/degreaser. Wipe a light coat of SAE 10 oil on the bed so the headstock, tailstock and tool rest base slide easily. Remove rust spots with a commercial rust remover.</li>
        <li>Extend the tailstock quill, wipe it, and apply light SAE 10 oil. Keep oil out of the taper bore.</li>
        <li>Blow out or vacuum sawdust from the headstock so it does not build up on belts and sheaves. Blow debris off the inverter but do not disassemble it. Wear eye protection with compressed air.</li>
        <li>Clean both Morse tapers frequently; taper cleaners are available from tool stores.</li>
        <li>Check the belt for wear or hard spots and replace as needed. Verify sheave alignment.</li>
        <li>Check that the headstock, tailstock and tool rest clamp firmly when locked; adjust the cams as needed.</li>
        <li>All other servicing should be done by an authorized service representative.</li>
      </ul>

